<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Thessalian League</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Larissa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of the cities of Thessaly, famous for its cavalry, which elected a war leader called the tagos and was later dominated by Macedon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> were ruled by landowning families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuadae">Aleuadae</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/larissa">Larissa</a>. By the 6th century BCE the cities formed a league with the neighbouring perioikoi peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleuas-the-red">Aleuas the Red</a> was credited with organising Thessaly into four districts, each supplying cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In wartime the league elected a tagos as commander. Thessalian cavalry was the best in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the economy rested on grain and horses worked by serfs called penestai. The Thessalians sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 480 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason-of-pherae">Jason of Pherae</a>, elected tagos c. 374 BCE, briefly made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> a great power before his murder in 370 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> was made archon of the league in the 340s BCE, and Macedonian kings held the post thereafter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> restored a Thessalian League centred on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/larissa">Larissa</a> in 196 BCE, and it continued under Roman control from 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 600 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden